WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Let’s talk about what everyone is talking about:
&nbsp;

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.080
the new coronavirus.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.600
Is this the Big One?

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.280
Honestly it’s hard to say

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.440
but it’s moving fast and it doesn’t look good.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.120
What started in the city of Wuhan in early December

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.320
has now grown into a global health emergency.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.400
And as the contagion spread beyond China so did the fear.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.240
I’m really afraid that the virus might infect me

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.680
or my family members.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.200
We all have questions. 

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.280
How contagious is this new virus?

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.640
What are the symptoms? 

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:32.960
And how do we stay safe?

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.160
So this is what we’re dealing with:

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.440
a virus they started calling 2019-nCoV.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.880
The N’s for novel, meaning new

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.360
and CoV stands for coronavirus.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:50.760
And this is what it looks like.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.840
It’s like viruses we’ve seen before

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.800
and comes from the same family as SARS and MERS.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.000
But we’ll explain the science in a bit.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.720
First let’s get a sense of how we got here.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.040
It started with a viral blip: 

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.520
a few cases of pneumonia in Wuhan. 

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.600
Then doctors noticed that many of the people 

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.000
who were sick had a connection 

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.760
to a market that sold fish and wildlife.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.120
From there the virus spread until tens of millions of people in China

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.560
 in China were put under quarantine.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.440
Wuhan was locked down first.  

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.080
”Flight has been cancelled.”

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.000
Airlines pulled flights and trains stopped running.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.640
Schools and businesses were closed

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.800
and people were told to stay indoors.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:32.840
And although some residents  

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.680
were making light of the lockdown

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.240
many are struggling to hang in there.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.320
They just don’t know how long this is all going to last.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.560
The virus has now been found in all of China’s 31 provinces.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.360
It’s put the country on high alert.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.440
In a rare move the government has closed

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.320
the Forbidden City here in Beijing

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.680
over health concerns related to the coronavirus.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.000
People are avoiding any kind of place where there's crowds.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.600
So therefore sports arenas, hotels, bars, restaurants

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.560
movie theatres are not going to be doing very well 

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:09.880
for quite some time.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.960
Stock markets took a dive and there’s talk about

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.640
the global economy being dragged down.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.120
So what do we know about coronaviruses?

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.040
Well they’re common and usually found in animals.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.600
But six of them were known to infect people.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.400
This new one takes that up to seven.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.160
Four of those strains are 
pretty harmless.  

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.520
And might cause things like a cold. 

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.040
The others are killers.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.600
Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome showed up in 2002

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.960
and killed one in 10 patients.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.880
Experts think SARS jumped the species barrier

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.360
from the civet cat to humans through bats.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.080
In 2012 it was the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.880
which kills roughly one in three people who get it. 

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.560
MERS is thought to have come from camels.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.760
Now SARS disappeared in 2004 

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.640
but a few cases of MERS did pop up recently.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.840
And now there’s this new strain

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.800
which we’re still trying to understand.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.920
It’s already killed a lot of people

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.400
but it’s too early to tell what the mortality rate is.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.280
We also know that early cases were linked 

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.640
to that market in Wuhan where we know live animals

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.240
were in close contact with people

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.520
which is not unusual in Chinese markets.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.280
It would’ve given that mutated virus

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.920
ample opportunity to find a human host.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.160
This virus, this novel coronavirus that we're seeing

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.680
out of Wuhan, is 96% similar to a virus carried by bats

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.120
that we have studied in China.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.120
So this also is a bat virus

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.960
and the big question is how did it get into this market?

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.600
One of the problems with this virus is that 

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.680
it's hard to tell if you have it.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.800
It might just feel like you have a cold.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.600
We know it can give you 
a cough

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.920
and a fever even shortness of breath.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.320
But one in five people get severely ill 

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.880
and have developed pneumonia, kidney failure. 

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.040
Some have died.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.800
So you don’t want to wait too long before you see a doctor.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.160
Ultimately how badly it 
affects you seems to

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.200
depend on how strong your body’s immune system is.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.800
Of the people who have so far died 

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.760
most of them were elderly 

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.640
and most of them had an underlying illness.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.240
So for the moment, you know, the doctors

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.840
the experts are telling us we do not need to be too alarmed

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.920
because at the moment we're not sure

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:24.000
 just how deadly this virus is. 

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.360
One thing we know is that the virus incubates between

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.320
two and 14 days beforea person

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.480
shows signs of being sick.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.240
But it’s been a struggle to understand 

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:33.760
how contagious it is. 

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.600
China’s National Health Commission said that

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.720
a person without symptoms is still infectious.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.240
Which is pretty scary.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.080
But the WHO backed away from that

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.600
and said the most common way of getting the virus

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.920
is from people who are already visibly sick.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.720
So how does it travel?

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.520
Scientists are working off clues based on

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.600
how SARS and MERS behave.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.440
With those two the virus travels from person to person

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.880
through droplets that can enter someone’s body

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.280
through their nose, mouth or even eyes.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.880
And that generally requires close contact.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:09.400
Best-case scenario is that it has a strong seasonal impact

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.000
and that as the climate warms up in China

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.280
it sort of dwindles away by itself.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.040
But I’m afraid that’s not very realistic. 

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.160
Something else we know is the virus’s genetic sequence

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.560
which China made public in the early days of the outbreak.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:26.760
This DNA map has helped scientists come up with

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.920
a test to diagnose the virus.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.120
It might also lead to a vaccine.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.880
A vaccine is of course the long-term solution

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.680
because developing one takes time

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.800
and costs millions of dollars.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.800
So right now it’s all about the emergency response.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.920
We’re talking about treating thousands of people

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.240
many of them suffering alone in hospital beds

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.160
away from their families and probably feeling pretty scared.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.120
The health workers taking care of them are too.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:01.360
And they’re overwhelmed.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.840
Across China medical supplies including diagnostic kits

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:14.040
have been running out. So has bed space.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.920
have been running out. So has bed space.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.960
In response the Chinese government built 

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.600
two field hospitals in Wuhan from scratch

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.080
in a matter of weeks.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.280
And one factory in Thailand put in overtime

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.200
 to make more surgical masks 

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.760
even though not all medical experts are convinced

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.120
they’re much protection in public.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.000
There’s a lot of fear and misinformation

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.080
out there right now

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:40.560
so while the scientists are working out  

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.160
how to contain the outbreak 

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.400
here’s the general advice from the experts.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.360
Stay away from people who are sick.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:49.320
If you’re ill see a doctor sooner rather than later. 

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.880
Cough into your elbow or a tissue, not your hands

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:53.600
to avoid spreading germs.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.520
And wash your hands. A lot. 

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.480
You know: the basics.

